{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"set -euo pipefail\nout='/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/reasoning_quality/run-20260426T075611Z/medium/site/spaces/alpha/runs/run-20260426T080129Z--cgg5yfl07w/semantic/ingest_extraction.json'\nmkdir -p \\\"\"'$(dirname \"$out\")\"\npython3 - <<'\"'PY' > \\\"\"'$out\"\nimport json\nobj = {\n  \"source_date_inference\": {\n    \"confidence\": \"unknown\",\n    \"date\": None,\n    \"origin\": \"unknown\",\n    \"rationale\": None\n  },\n  \"source\": {\n    \"display_title\": \"Why Grounding Entails Necessity\",\n    \"authors\": [\"Kelly Trogdon\"]\n  },\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"If a fact [p] is fully grounded in a plurality of facts D, then it is metaphysically necessary that the conjunction of the propositions corresponding to D entails p.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Full grounds entail p\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"necessity: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then 䊐(∧D → p).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The relevant sense in which grounding is necessary is the third-style modal consequence that the grounds entail the grounded proposition, not that the grounding relation itself recurs whenever the grounds obtain.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Necessity concerns modal consequence\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Three possibilities to consider: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then it’s metaphysically necessary that (i) if ∧D then [p] is grounded in D; (ii) if ∧D then something or other is grounded in D; or (iii) if ∧D then p.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The truth of grounding necessity would make maximalist truthmaker theories for negative existentials harder to defend because candidate truthmakers would need to entail the relevant negative existential truths.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Necessity constrains truthmakers\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"If necessity is true, however, the potential truthmakers for <there are no unicorns> become increasingly baroque, appealing to entities such as totality propositions and absences.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The difference between full grounding and merely partial grounding is explanatory rather than purely modal, because necessary truths can have mere partial grounds while still being modally entailed by those grounds.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Fullness is explanatory\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Moral: the distinction between full and mere partial grounding is an explanatory distinction rather than a purely modal one.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"A fact [p] is fully grounded in D only if the connecting question asking why [p] obtains given D lacks cognitive significance.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Grounding requires insignificance\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"significance: [p] is (fully) grounded in D only if the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"A connecting question is cognitively significant exactly when full information about the relevant facts, plurality, and entities would still not make the question lose substantive content for any rational individual.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Cognitive significance persists\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Let a connecting question of the form ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each of the members of D obtains?’ be cognitively significant just in case it’s metaphysically necessary that, for any rational individual, being fully informed about the nature of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, and the entities these facts involve doesn’t suffice for the question to lack substantive content for that individual.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"When the connecting questions for [p] and D lack cognitive significance, Fine-style essential truths concerning the relevant facts, their plurality, or involved entities explain why ∧D entails p.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Essence explains entailment\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"essence: the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance only if there are certain essential truths characteristic of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"The argument for grounding necessity combines full grounding, the significance principle, the essence principle, and the metaphysical necessity of essential truths to derive □(∧D → p).\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Essence yields necessity\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"(1) Suppose that [p] is (fully) grounded in D. (2) By (1) and significance, the connecting question ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each member of D obtains?’ lacks cognitive significance. (3) By (2) and essence, there are essential truths about [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p. (4) The essential truths are metaphysically necessary. (5) By (3) and (4), 䊐(∧D → p).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Paradigm cases such as the grounding of conjunctions and disjunctions support the significance principle because their connecting questions lack substantive content even with little background knowledge.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Paradigms support significance\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"Paradigm cases of grounding have the relevant epistemic feature, two of which we’ve already discussed: the grounding of [p∧q] in [p], [q], and the grounding of [p⁄q] in [p], for some suitable p and q.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Apparent counterexamples to grounding necessity fail either because they mistake merely partial grounds for full grounds or because they mistake a non-ground for a ground.\",\n      \"short_title\": \"Counterexamples misidentify grounds\",\n      \"evidence_excerpts\": [\n        \"When faced with a case in which it seems that [p] is fully grounded in D yet ~䊐(∧D → p), the advocate of necessity should check to see if there are additional explanatorily relevant facts P such that 䊐(∧(D, P) → p).\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_items\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-necessity-thesis--18b71a159e80\",\n      \"title\": \"Necessity Thesis Formula\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"Where ‘䊐’ formalizes metaphysical necessity, consider, then, the following thesis: necessity: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then 䊐(∧D → p).\",\n      \"overview\": \"This formal statement fixes the paper’s target thesis. It identifies the relevant modal consequence of full grounding: the propositions corresponding to a full ground must metaphysically necessitate the proposition corresponding to what is grounded. The formula also limits the claim to full grounds rather than partial explanatory factors.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"0\", \"1\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"p.466\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-truthmaker-unicorns--a7e502b87698\",\n      \"title\": \"Truthmaker Constraint Example\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"If necessity is true, however, the potential truthmakers for <there are no unicorns> become increasingly baroque, appealing to entities such as totality propositions and absences. Hence, while maximalism is on fairly firm ground with respect to negative existential truths provided that necessity is false, if necessity is instead true this might not be the case.\",\n      \"overview\": \"The negative-existential example shows why the modal status of grounding matters outside the internal theory of grounding. If truthmaking is analyzed through full grounding, then grounding necessity restricts acceptable truthmakers to entities or facts whose existence entails the truth made true. That makes familiar candidates for negative existentials more controversial.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"2\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"pp.466-467\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-partial-grounding-distinction--69315a7e502b\",\n      \"title\": \"Partial Grounding Distinction\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"If [p] is merely partially grounded in D and <p> is necessarily true, it’s a trivial matter that 䊐(∧D → p). Consider again [p∧q]. Suppose that <p> is a necessary truth, as is <q>. For some suitable p and q, [p∧q] is merely partially grounded in [p], despite the fact that <p∧q> is true in all possible worlds. Moral: the distinction between full and mere partial grounding is an explanatory distinction rather than a purely modal one.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This passage blocks an easy definitional route to necessity. Modal entailment alone cannot distinguish full from partial grounds, because necessary truths make entailment trivial even when an explanans is incomplete. The argument therefore motivates Trogdon’s later appeal to cognitive significance and essence rather than a purely modal analysis of full grounding.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"3\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"pp.467-468\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-significance-principle--12009a7e502b\",\n      \"title\": \"Significance Principle\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"significance: [p] is (fully) grounded in D only if the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance.\",\n      \"overview\": \"The significance principle supplies the first substantive premise of the central argument. It links full grounding to a broadly epistemic condition: once the relevant entities and facts are properly grasped, the question why the grounded fact obtains given the grounds should not remain a live substantive explanatory demand.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"model_constraint\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"4\", \"8\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"p.467\", \"p.472\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-cognitive-significance-definition--46646712009aa\",\n      \"title\": \"Cognitive Significance Definition\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"Let a connecting question of the form ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each of the members of D obtains?’ be cognitively significant just in case it’s metaphysically necessary that, for any rational individual, being fully informed about the nature of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, and the entities these facts involve doesn’t suffice for the question to lack substantive content for that individual.\",\n      \"overview\": \"This definition clarifies the epistemic notion used in the argument. Cognitive significance is not merely current ignorance by a particular person; it concerns whether full information about the relevant natures would still leave a rational individual with a substantive explanatory question. That helps Trogdon distinguish the explanatory gap and open question cases from ordinary explanatory incompleteness.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"model_constraint\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"5\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"pp.469-470\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-essence-principle--94a7e502b876\",\n      \"title\": \"Essence Principle\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"essence: the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance only if there are certain essential truths characteristic of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p.\",\n      \"overview\": \"The essence principle supplies the bridge from an epistemic constraint to modal entailment. Trogdon’s idea is that a connecting question ceases to have substantive content because grasping the nature of the relevant facts or entities reveals an essential truth according to which the grounds suffice for the grounded proposition.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"6\", \"7\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"p.467\", \"p.473\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-five-step-necessity-proof--201312009a7e\",\n      \"title\": \"Five Step Necessity Proof\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"(1) Suppose that [p] is (fully) grounded in D. (2) By (1) and significance, the connecting question ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each member of D obtains?’ lacks cognitive significance. (3) By (2) and essence, there are essential truths about [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p. (4) The essential truths are metaphysically necessary. (5) By (3) and (4), 䊐(∧D → p).\",\n      \"overview\": \"This proof step assembles the article’s core reasoning in explicit form. Full grounding triggers the significance condition; lack of cognitive significance is explained by essential truths; essential truths are metaphysically necessary; therefore the full grounds metaphysically necessitate the grounded proposition. The conclusion depends on both substantive principles, not on defining full grounding modally.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"proof_step\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"0\", \"4\", \"6\", \"7\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"p.467\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"evidence-counterexample-diagnosis--46548518b71a\",\n      \"title\": \"Counterexample Diagnosis\",\n      \"excerpt\": \"If such additional facts are available, we have reason to believe that D is a mere partial ground that was misidentified as a full ground; in such a case, [p] instead is fully grounded in D, P. Each of the three cases set out above are such that additional explanatorily relevant facts are available that meet the modal entailment condition: what are identified as enabling conditions in the first case, a totality fact – the fact to the effect that a, b, etc. are everything that exists – in the second case, and the fact that Socrates and Xanthippe were married in the third case.\",\n      \"overview\": \"The response to counterexamples explains how necessity can survive cases where an alleged ground does not entail its explanandum. Trogdon treats several examples as incomplete explanations: once enabling conditions, totality facts, or marriage facts are included, the modal entailment condition is restored. The strategy also leaves room for diagnosing some alleged grounds as non-grounds rather than partial grounds.\",\n      \"evidence_type\": \"formal_argument\",\n      \"claim_refs\": [\"9\"],\n      \"source_id\": \"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\",\n      \"page_refs\": [\"p.478\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"relations\": [\n    {\"type\": \"supports\", \"src\": \"4\", \"dst\": \"7\", \"description\": \"The significance principle is the first substantive premise in the proof of grounding necessity.\"},\n    {\"type\": \"supports\", \"src\": \"6\", \"dst\": \"7\", \"description\": \"The essence principle supplies the route from lack of cognitive significance to entailment.\"},\n    {\"type\": \"supports\", \"src\": \"3\", \"dst\": \"4\", \"description\": \"The explanatory nature of full grounding motivates using cognitive significance rather than purely modal fullness.\"},\n    {\"type\": \"qualifies\", \"src\": \"9\", \"dst\": \"0\", \"description\": \"The counterexample discussion narrows necessity to genuine full grounds and excludes partial grounds or non-grounds.\"}\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"Kelly Trogdon argues that full grounding is necessary in the sense that, whenever [p] is fully grounded in D, the propositions corresponding to D metaphysically entail p. The case turns on a broadly epistemic significance principle and a Fine-style essence principle: full grounding requires its connecting question to lack cognitive significance, and that lack is explained by essential truths that make ∧D entail p.\",\n  \"warnings\": [\n    {\"code\": \"missing_publication_date\", \"message\": \"Publication date could not be resolved from the source record; source_date_inference.date remains null.\"}\n  ],\n  \"source_dossier\": {\n    \"summary_short\": \"Kelly Trogdon defends the thesis that full grounding is necessary: if [p] is fully grounded in D, then the conjunction of the propositions corresponding to D metaphysically entails p. The article argues that this is not because grounding is a merely modal relation, but because full grounding is constrained by cognitive significance and Fine-style essence. Apparent counterexamples are treated as cases of partial grounding or non-grounding.\",\n    \"summary_long\": \"Grounding: Necessary or Contingent? asks whether grounding has modal consequences even though grounding itself is not reducible to modality. Trogdon distinguishes several ways a relation might be necessary and focuses on the one most relevant to grounding: if a fact [p] is fully grounded in a plurality of facts D, then it is metaphysically necessary that if all the propositions corresponding to D obtain, p obtains. This matters for broader metaphysics because many positions are stated in grounding vocabulary. For example, if truthmaking is analyzed as grounding, then the necessity of grounding constrains which entities can make negative existential truths true. The paper rejects two quick paths to the thesis: grounding is not necessary merely because determination relations are necessary, and the full/partial grounding distinction cannot be captured by modal entailment alone, since necessary truths can be partially grounded while still being trivially entailed. Trogdon instead builds a two-premise argument. The first premise, significance, says that full grounding requires the relevant connecting question to lack cognitive significance. A connecting question asks why [p] should obtain given the facts in D. The discussion contrasts ordinary cases where more knowledge would dissolve the question, such as fragility or shape examples, with cases like phenomenal consciousness or moral rightness where the question appears to retain substantive force even under full information. The second premise, essence, says that when a connecting question lacks cognitive significance, this is because essential truths concerning the relevant facts, their plurality, or involved entities reveal that ∧D entails p. Drawing on Fine’s Aristotelian conception of essence, Trogdon treats essential truths as metaphysically necessary and not grounded in worldly modal facts. The resulting proof is compact: full grounding implies lack of cognitive significance; lack of cognitive significance implies essential truths according to which ∧D entails p; essential truths are metaphysically necessary; therefore □(∧D → p). The conclusion is then protected against putative counterexamples by arguing that many alleged full grounds are only partial grounds needing additional explanatorily relevant facts, while at least one substance-mode case misidentifies a non-ground as a ground.\",\n    \"sections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Target Modal Thesis\",\n        \"body\": \"The article isolates a specific necessity claim for grounding: full grounds need not guarantee that the same grounding relation recurs, but they must guarantee the truth of what they ground. In formal terms, if [p] is fully grounded in D, then □(∧D → p). This makes grounding modally consequential without reducing it to a modal relation.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\"0\", \"1\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Why The Thesis Matters\",\n        \"body\": \"Trogdon uses truthmaker maximalism to show the stakes. If truthmaking is understood through grounding, then a truthmaker for a negative existential must entail that negative truth when grounding necessity holds. This pushes theorists toward more demanding truthmakers, such as totality propositions or absences, instead of easy positive-fact candidates.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\"2\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Against Purely Modal Fullness\",\n        \"body\": \"The paper denies that full grounding can be defined simply by modal entailment. Necessary truths make entailment too cheap: a partial ground can entail a necessary conjunction merely because the conclusion is necessary. Fullness is therefore an explanatory status, and the argument must explain why genuine full grounds nevertheless entail what they ground.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\"3\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Cognitive Significance Constraint\",\n        \"body\": \"The significance principle connects grounding to the status of explanatory questions. If D fully grounds [p], then the question why [p] obtains given D should not remain substantively live for a suitably informed rational individual. This is an objective epistemic constraint, not a report about any particular person’s ignorance or psychology.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\"4\", \"5\", \"8\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Essence Bridges To Modality\",\n        \"body\": \"The essence principle explains why the disappearance of a connecting question yields entailment. On Trogdon’s Finean picture, grasping what the relevant facts, plurality, or entities are can reveal essential truths according to which the grounds suffice for p. Since essential truths are metaphysically necessary, they transmit necessity to the grounding connection.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\"6\", \"7\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Limits And Counterexamples\",\n        \"body\": \"The conclusion applies to full grounds, so counterexamples must show a genuine full ground that does not entail its explanandum. Trogdon argues that obligation, universal generalization, and widowhood cases omit enabling, totality, or background facts. The rose redness case is diagnosed more strongly as mistaking a non-ground for a ground.\",\n        \"grounding_claim_ids\": [\"0\", \"9\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\nprint(json.dumps(obj, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))\nPY'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
